<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
 <!ENTITY name      "aegis.mfa">
 <!ENTITY author    "Lazaros Chalkidis">
 <!ENTITY version   "2026.10.26">
 <!ENTITY branch    "main">
 <!ENTITY gitURL    "https://github.com/Lazaros-Chalkidis/unraid-aegis-mfa">
 <!ENTITY pluginURL "&gitURL;/releases/download/&version;/&name;-&version;.txz">
 <!ENTITY selfURL   "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Lazaros-Chalkidis/unraid-aegis-mfa/&branch;/&name;.plg">
 <!ENTITY md5       "33b757903459f148d1c0a4f30767f958">
 <!ENTITY launch    "Settings/AegisMfa">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" Title="Aegis MFA" author="&author;" version="&version;"
        pluginURL="&selfURL;" launch="&launch;"
        icon="icon-aegismfa"
        min="7.3.1"
        support="https://github.com/Lazaros-Chalkidis/unraid-aegis-mfa/issues">

<DESCRIPTION>
<![CDATA[
Two-factor authentication for the Unraid webGUI. TOTP codes from any authenticator app, per user, with backup codes, trusted LAN bypass, and lockout protection. Fail-open by design: a fault can never lock you out.
]]>
</DESCRIPTION>

<CHANGES>
<![CDATA[

# Aegis MFA

## Version 2026.10.26

### Added
- Passkeys as a second factor: face/fingerprint/PIN or a hardware security key, alongside or instead of an authenticator app. Verified by a from-scratch WebAuthn implementation (ES256 only, no third-party library), consistent with the rest of the codebase
- A new setup step lets you choose an authenticator app or a passkey; backup codes are generated and required either way, unchanged from before
- Settings > Aegis MFA gets a Passkeys section: the HTTPS address a passkey is bound to, and per-account add/rename/remove, with a guard against removing an account's only remaining second factor
- The login challenge offers "Use a passkey instead" whenever one is registered and the address matches
- A "behind an HTTPS-terminating reverse proxy" toggle for the passkey address, for setups like Nginx Proxy Manager where Unraid itself only ever sees the plain-HTTP side of the connection

### Security
- A passkey's signature counter must increase each time it's used, rejecting a cloned authenticator
- Passkey attempts count against the same per-IP lockout as TOTP codes

### Fixed
- The field for typing a code back under New codes showed as a full-width line instead of a small box, because Unraid's own input style took over

Contributed by [Sebastian Oehm](https://github.com/SebastianOehm)

---

### Fixed
- Failed today counted only the last five minutes and reset after a correct code, it now counts the whole day
- Removing a passkey checks that another factor remains at the moment of removal, not from an earlier read

## Version 2026.07.31

### Security
- The settings page now needs a code like every other page. It has the Disable button on it, so the password alone was enough to switch MFA off
- Every action on the settings page carries its own CSRF token
- Two copies of the same code sent at the same moment can no longer both get in
- Setup links and the account name in your authenticator no longer come from a header a visitor can set
- `aegis-mfa enforce` rejects anything that is not a plain number of hours
- The failure counter has an upper bound, so a flood from many addresses cannot fill memory

### Added
- Lockout history: a section listing blocked addresses with the failure count, when they were locked and how long is left. Unlock frees one now, Delete drops a spent row, Clear removes the expired ones
- Keep history switch: blocked addresses survive a reboot. Nothing is written while requests come in, the ten-minute cron saves a snapshot and only when something changed
- The page warns you when the browser you are on is on the trusted list, since an exempt browser looked exactly like a protected one
- Accounts that cannot sign in to the webGUI, share users, are marked as such and left out of the count.

### Changed
- New backup codes now work like the first set: shown once, downloadable, and confirmed by typing one back. Your current codes keep working until you confirm, so closing the page halfway no longer leaves you with none
- Uninstalling keeps your settings and enrolments, and takes only its own files. A reinstall picks them up with MFA switched off. The command to erase everything is printed at the end
- Grace period defaults to two days instead of seven on new installs. An existing setting is left alone
- The Locked out text now explains why SSH still works, gives the exact command, and separates USB boot from internal boot
- The syslog line says which credential was used, `app code` or `backup code` with how many are left

### Fixed
- MFA is enabled but nothing to look at: the notice promised a dry run even when the server went straight to enforcing
- `aegis-mfa status` reported no enrolled accounts on a server that had them
- A half-finished code re-issue reappeared after a full uninstall, offering codes for an account that no longer existed
- Background requests received the login page instead of an answer, which showed up as stray text at the top of the webGUI
- On the white and azure themes the title, the notice and the summary cards could turn invisible
- Downloaded packages piled up on the boot device, one per release
- The stylesheet could stay cached after an update

## Version 2026.07.25

### Improvements
- The shield icon is drawn as a thin outline with a filled keyhole, in the header, on the settings page, in the Plugins list and in the CA artwork, which stays blue
- White and azure get darker overrides for the warning, locked, power, apply and link colours that were hard to read on light backgrounds

## Version 2026.07.24

### Improvements
- Wording changes for clearer phrasing

## Version 2026.07.21

### Fixed
- Support links now point to GitHub Issues, and the README wording was tightened

## Version 2026.07.19

### First release
- TOTP two-factor authentication for the Unraid webGUI, on top of the existing password login
- Any authenticator app works: Google Authenticator, Authy, Bitwarden, whatever you already use
- Root only: root is the only account the Unraid webGUI accepts, and enrols with its own secret and its own backup codes
- Setup wizard: scan a QR, confirm a code, save ten backup codes and type one back to finish
- The QR is generated on the server, the secret never leaves the machine
- Ten single-use backup codes, hashed on disk, with a remaining count in the settings
- Trusted LAN: no code prompt from networks you list, the password is still required. Your own address is shown ready to add
- Grace period: root gets a set number of days to enrol before setup becomes mandatory
- Dry-run: for the first 24 hours nothing is blocked, you see the prompts and failures are only logged, then you enforce when ready
- Lockout: repeated wrong codes lock the source IP out. Counters live in RAM, so brute force never wears the flash drive
- A code that was already used is refused, even inside its own 30-second window
- A failed code shows the server's own time, the usual culprit when codes suddenly stop matching
- Admin actions on the settings page: one-time setup link, fresh backup codes, reset
- Fail-open by design: whatever breaks, corrupt config, a missing file, an Unraid update, the result is password-only login, never a locked door
- The two Unraid login files are checked before they are touched, an unrecognised layout is refused instead of guessed at
- Self-healing: a boot hook and a ten-minute cron re-apply the login patches if an Unraid update removes them
- An Unraid notification fires when MFA is on but the patches are missing, and once more when protection returns
- Ways back in: backup codes, `aegis-mfa disable` over SSH, a `DISABLE.flag` file on the flash, or `recover.sh` on the flash, pure bash, works even with PHP broken
- CLI: `aegis-mfa` covers status, enable, disable, enforce and a preflight report that rehearses the patches before you enable anything
- Every success and failure lands in the syslog with the account and the source IP
- The challenge and the wizard match the Unraid login card, the settings follow the Aegis styling, all four webGUI themes supported
- A notice warns when your Unraid release has not been tested with the plugin yet
- Needs Unraid 7.3.1 or newer, enforced at install
]]>
</CHANGES>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
UNRAID_VER=$(sed -n "s/^version=\"\(.*\)\"/\1/p" /etc/unraid-version 2>/dev/null)
MIN_VER="7.3.1"
if [ -z "$UNRAID_VER" ]; then
    echo "Cannot read /etc/unraid-version. Installation aborted."
    exit 1
fi
LOWEST=$(printf "%s\n%s\n" "$UNRAID_VER" "$MIN_VER" | sort -V | head -1)
TOO_OLD=0
if [ "$UNRAID_VER" != "$MIN_VER" ]; then
    if [ "$LOWEST" != "$MIN_VER" ]; then
        TOO_OLD=1
    fi
fi
if [ "$TOO_OLD" = "1" ]; then
    echo ""
    echo "----------------------------------------------------"
    echo " Aegis MFA requires Unraid ${MIN_VER} or newer."
    echo " This server runs ${UNRAID_VER}."
    echo ""
    echo " Older releases ship different PHP and nginx internals,"
    echo " and this plugin patches the login path. Installing on"
    echo " an unverified base is not safe, so it is refused."
    echo ""
    echo " Installation aborted. Nothing was changed."
    echo "----------------------------------------------------"
    echo ""
    exit 1
fi
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-&version;.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
  <URL>&pluginURL;</URL>
  <MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
# Fix ownership and permissions
chown -R root:root /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;
find /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name; -type d -exec chmod 755 {} \;
find /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name; -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;
find /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name; -name "*.sh" -exec chmod 755 {} \;

# no-extension files chmodded by name, emhttpd only runs an executable hook
chmod 755 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/event/started
chmod 755 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/scripts/aegis-mfa
chmod 755 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/scripts/aegis-mfa-cron

# the CLI on PATH is how a locked-out admin gets back in: ssh, then aegis-mfa disable
ln -sf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/scripts/aegis-mfa /usr/local/sbin/aegis-mfa

# second re-apply mechanism next to the boot hook. no dot in the filename, cron.d skips dotted files on some builds
install -m 0644 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/&name;.cron /etc/cron.d/aegis-mfa
/etc/rc.d/rc.crond restart >/dev/null 2>/dev/null

# first install only, an upgrade leaves the existing config exactly as it is. no merge step,
# the PHP layer deep-merges defaults at read time. copied from the package, a heredoc is not valid XML here
mkdir -p /boot/config/plugins/&name;
if [ ! -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/config.json ]; then
    cp /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/config.default.json /boot/config/plugins/&name;/config.json
fi

# secrets.json is never written here, an upgrade must not touch a single byte of it

# unraid reinstalls from the local package at boot, so the current one stays and older ones go
for pkg in /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-*.txz; do
  if [ -f "$pkg" ]; then
    if [ "$pkg" != "/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-&version;.txz" ]; then
      rm -f "$pkg"
      echo " Removed old package $(basename "$pkg")."
    fi
  fi
done

# leftover from the removed checksum list, plugin-managed and unread. logged, not removed quietly
if [ -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/compat.json ]; then
    rm -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/compat.json
    logger -t aegis-mfa "removed obsolete compat.json from the boot device"
    echo " Removed obsolete compat.json (tested-release list, no longer used)."
fi

# the pure-bash recovery script goes on the flash: reachable with the plugin folder gone,
# working with PHP broken. overwritten every install to stay current
cp -f /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/scripts/aegis-mfa-recover.sh \
      /boot/config/plugins/&name;/recover.sh 2>/dev/null
chmod 0755 /boot/config/plugins/&name;/recover.sh 2>/dev/null

# reset opcache so the login path runs the upgraded files, not stale bytecode
php -r "if (function_exists(\"opcache_reset\")) opcache_reset();" >/dev/null 2>/dev/null


# bring the patches in line with the saved setting: no-op on first install, on an upgrade with MFA
# on it re-points the hooks at this build. anything off removes the patches, never half-wired
php -r "require \"/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/include/AegisMfaInstall.php\"; \$r = aegis_mfa_reconcile_patches(); echo \"patch state: \" . (\$r[\"state\"] ?? \"unknown\") . PHP_EOL;" 2>/dev/null

echo ""
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo " &name; (&branch; build) installed successfully."
echo " Version : &version;"
echo " Settings: Settings > Aegis MFA"
echo ""
echo " MFA is OFF until you enable it. Enabling starts a"
echo " 24h dry-run so you can confirm it works before it"
echo " is enforced."
echo ""
echo " Locked out? From SSH or the console:  aegis-mfa disable"
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
# 1. Un-patch the login files FIRST, while our code still exists, and turn the saved switch off with them
SWITCH_OFF=0
if [[ -f /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/include/AegisMfaInstall.php ]]; then
    if php -r "require \"/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/include/AegisMfaInstall.php\"; require \"/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/include/AegisMfaAdmin.php\"; \$r = aegis_mfa_uninstall(); echo \"login files restored: \" . (\$r[\"state\"] ?? \"unknown\") . PHP_EOL; exit(aegis_mfa_set_enabled(false) ? 0 : 1);" 2>/dev/null; then
        SWITCH_OFF=1
    fi
fi

# 2. drop the CLI symlink and the cron entry
rm -f /usr/local/sbin/aegis-mfa
rm -f /etc/cron.d/aegis-mfa
/etc/rc.d/rc.crond restart >/dev/null 2>/dev/null

# 3. drop the in-RAM lockout counters
rm -rf /var/local/&name;

# 4. now the package and the plugin folder can go
removepkg &name;-&version;
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;

# 5. only our own files leave the boot device: one unconfirmed click is not consent to drop enrolments
rm -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/recover.sh
rm -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-*.txz
rm -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/lockouts.json

# the flag is the fallback for a switch we could not write, so it only stays behind when PHP did not run
if [[ "$SWITCH_OFF" == "1" ]]; then
    rm -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/DISABLE.flag
fi
rmdir /boot/config/plugins/&name; 2>/dev/null

# clear opcache so the restored login files are the ones that run
php -r "if (function_exists(\"opcache_reset\")) opcache_reset();" >/dev/null 2>/dev/null

echo ""
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo " &name; has been removed."
echo " The Unraid login files were restored to stock and"
echo " password-only login is active again."
echo ""
echo " Your settings and 2FA enrolments were left in place:"
echo "   /boot/config/plugins/&name;/"
echo " A reinstall picks them up, with MFA switched off until"
echo " you turn it back on. To erase them instead, including"
echo " the TOTP secrets and the backup codes:"
echo "   rm -rf /boot/config/plugins/&name;"
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
